    "result_text": "All 21 tests pass and the build is green. The implementation is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented address normalization applied at owner creation in `OwnerRestControllerV1` (the only place owners are created \u2014 the V2 controller is read-only).\n\n**Changes to `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`:**\n\n1. **New `normalizeAddress` method** \u2014 trims and collapses internal whitespace to single spaces, upper-cases, and expands common street-type abbreviations token-by-token (`ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE`). A `null` address normalizes to the empty string. A static `ADDRESS_ABBREVIATIONS` map holds the expansions; token-based lookup avoids mangling substrings (e.g. `EAST` is untouched).\n\n2. **Store and return the normalized address** \u2014 `addOwner` sets `owner.setAddress(normalizedAddress)` before persisting, so the canonical form is stored and returned.\n\n3. **Required-field check now uses the normalized form** \u2014 the `address` \"missing field\" check runs against `normalizeAddress(...)`, so a value that is blank after normalization (e.g. whitespace-only) is rejected with 400.\n\n4. **Comparisons use the normalized form** \u2014 because the stored address is now the normalized canonical value, household duplicate detection (`findHouseholdMembers`) and the shared household id (`generateHouseholdId`) \u2014 which both operate on `owner.getAddress()` and against existing owners' stored addresses \u2014 compare normalized addresses. So `\"12 Main St\"`, `\"12  main  street\"`, and `\"12  MAIN  ST\"` all resolve to the same household and household id.\n\nThe three new tests (`Cp10`, `Cp11`, `Cp12`) and all 21 tests in the suite pass.",
